# ollama stack — manual setup

Migrates the host's native `ollama.service` into Docker with GPU
passthrough, then exposes it publicly behind an API key via
nginx-proxy-manager.

## 0. Prerequisites (already done on this host)

```bash
sudo pacman -S nvidia-container-toolkit
sudo nvidia-ctk cdi generate --output=/etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml
```

Verify CDI passthrough works before going further:

```bash
docker run --rm --device nvidia.com/gpu=all nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04 nvidia-smi -L
```

Should print your GPU. If it instead errors `AMD CDI spec not found`,
you're hitting the same multi-vendor CDI probe issue this compose file
works around — see CONTEXT.md. Don't try `docker run --gpus all` here, it
hits the same error; the CDI `--device nvidia.com/gpu=all` form is what
actually works on this host's Docker version.

## 1. Seed model data from the host install

The host's native install has ~23GB already pulled
(`/usr/share/ollama/.ollama`, root-only 700 perms) — copy it in instead of
re-pulling over the network:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/storage/homelab/homelab-conf/ollama/data
sudo rsync -a --info=progress2 /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/ ~/storage/homelab/homelab-conf/ollama/data/
sudo chown -R "$(id -u):$(id -g)" ~/storage/homelab/homelab-conf/ollama/data
```

(The container runs as root itself, so ownership doesn't strictly matter
for the container to read it — this chown is just so the directory is
manageable from your own user afterward.)

## 2. Bring up the stack

```bash
cd ~/homelab/containers
./up-all-stacks.sh ollama
```

Verify it's using the GPU, not falling back to CPU:

```bash
docker logs ollama 2>&1 | grep -i -E "gpu|cuda|vram"
docker exec ollama ollama list      # should show qwen3.6:35b etc. from the copied data
docker exec ollama ollama ps        # after a test prompt, confirms it's running on GPU not CPU
```

Quick end-to-end test from the host, over the edge network:

```bash
docker run --rm --network edge curlimages/curl -s http://ollama:11434/api/generate \
  -d '{"model": "qwen3.6:35b", "prompt": "say hi in 5 words", "stream": false}'
```

## 3. Point webui at it

Already changed in `containers/webui/docker-compose.yml`
(`OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434`). Recreate it so the change takes
effect:

```bash
./up-all-stacks.sh webui
```

Open `chat.smugiwara.com` and confirm models list and a chat completes.

## 4. Public endpoint + API key (nginx-proxy-manager)

Reach the NPM admin UI (see `containers/nginx-proxy-manager/.setup` for
the SSH tunnel). Generate a key first:

```bash
openssl rand -hex 32
```

**Proxy Hosts → Add Proxy Host**:

- Domain: `infer.smugiwara.com`
- Scheme: `http`, Forward host/port: `ollama:11434`
- **SSL tab**: request a new Let's Encrypt certificate, force SSL, HTTP/2
- **Advanced tab** — paste this, with your generated key in place of
  `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KEY`:

```nginx
if ($http_authorization != "Bearer REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KEY") {
    return 401;
}
proxy_read_timeout 600s;
proxy_buffering off;
```

(`proxy_buffering off` matters here — Ollama streams tokens as they're
generated, and NPM's default buffering would hold the whole response
until it's complete before forwarding it, defeating streaming clients.)

Save the key itself somewhere durable (password manager) — it lives only
in NPM's own config now (`homelab-conf/nginx-proxy-manager/data`, not
tracked in git), so it isn't recoverable from this repo if lost, only
rotatable (edit the Advanced tab snippet, distribute the new key to
whatever calls this endpoint).

Verify from outside the LAN (e.g. phone on cellular data, or `curl` from
another machine):

```bash
# without a key — expect 401
curl -i https://infer.smugiwara.com/api/tags

# with the key — expect 200 + model list
curl -s https://infer.smugiwara.com/api/tags \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KEY"
```

Any OpenAI/Ollama-compatible client pointed at
`https://infer.smugiwara.com` with that bearer token works the same way
(Ollama's `/v1/chat/completions` OpenAI-compatible endpoint included).

## 5. Retire the host's native ollama.service

Only once steps 2–4 are verified working:

```bash
sudo systemctl disable --now ollama.service
```

Confirm the GPU is now only claimed by the container:

```bash
nvidia-smi   # should show the `ollama` container's process, not a bare `ollama` binary
```

The host's `/usr/share/ollama/.ollama` data is untouched (only copied
from, not moved) — safe to leave as a rollback path, or delete later once
you're confident in the Docker version:

```bash
sudo rm -rf /usr/share/ollama/.ollama
```
